#Identify the syntax it is "print(chr(chr_value))" where chr_value is the ASCII value of the character you want to print. in py

Similarly in JAVA:
currentChar = 65
System.out.print((char) currentChar + " ");
here we need to convert 
In Java, (char) is known as a type casting operator(specifically, explicit type casting or narrowing conversion).
It is used to forcefully convert one data type into another.
In your code, currentChar is declared as an integer: int currentChar = 65;.If you run System.out.print(currentChar);, Java will literally print the number 65.By placing (char) right in front of it, you tell Java: "Do not treat this number as an integer. Look up its value on the ASCII/Unicode table and treat it as a text character instead."

